WEBVTT

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.890
In 1965, G. Evelyn Hutchinson described nature as the ecological theater:

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:10.360
the stage on which the evolutionary play is performed.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:14.480
Every question in ecology asks some version of the same thing.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.670
Why are these organisms here, in these numbers, alongside these neighbors?

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:23.507
Ecologists answer with theory: a toolkit of models for growth,

00:00:23.507 --> 00:00:27.660
niches, competition, predation, and movement across landscapes.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:34.120
In this program we build that toolkit, from a single population up to whole metacommunities.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:36.630
Watch the lexicon on the right of the screen.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:40.350
Every numbered term is one an examiner may ask you to define.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:45.480
Chapter one.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:49.690
Population dynamics: the rules by which numbers change through time.

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.860
Begin with a single population and no limits.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:01.833
If each individual has a constant per-capita birth rate, b, and death rate, d,

00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:09.080
the population grows as dN/dt = rN, where r = b − d is the intrinsic rate of increase.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:14.627
The result is exponential growth: a J-shaped curve in which the population

00:01:14.627 --> 00:01:19.100
doubles every ln 2 / r time units, no matter how large it already is.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:27.760
Species that breed in discrete pulses follow the geometric model, N(t+1) = λN(t).

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:31.690
Lambda, the finite rate of increase, equals e^r.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:36.870
When λ > 1 the population grows, when λ < 1 it declines,

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.370
and when λ = 1 it simply replaces itself.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:46.520
Both models are density-independent: per-capita rates do not change with crowding.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:52.008
No population sustains this for long, but it is the baseline every other model modifies,

00:01:52.008 --> 00:01:56.750
and it describes invasions and recoveries surprisingly well in their early phases.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:00.900
No population grows forever.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:07.470
As density rises, resources dwindle and per-capita growth declines: density dependence.

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:14.600
Its simplest form is the logistic model, dN/dt = rN(1 − N/K).

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:19.490
K is the carrying capacity, the density at which births balance deaths.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:24.623
Population growth is fastest at K/2, the inflection point of the S-shaped curve,

00:02:24.623 --> 00:02:29.120
which is why classic fisheries models place maximum sustainable yield there.

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:33.570
Density dependence can act on births, deaths, or both.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:37.609
It is compensatory when recruitment levels off at high density,

00:02:37.609 --> 00:02:43.385
as in the Beverton–Holt model, and overcompensatory when recruitment actually falls at high density,

00:02:43.385 --> 00:02:47.140
as in the Ricker model, which can produce boom-and-bust dynamics.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:50.630
At very low density the relationship can reverse.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:54.941
Under an Allee effect, per-capita growth increases with density,

00:02:54.941 --> 00:02:58.240
because mates are hard to find or group defenses fail.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.488
A strong Allee effect creates a critical threshold below

00:03:01.488 --> 00:03:03.930
which the population declines toward extinction.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.630
Density dependence rarely acts instantly.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:12.713
If growth responds to density at some time τ in the past,

00:03:12.713 --> 00:03:15.880
a population can overshoot its carrying capacity.

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:20.878
In Hutchinson’s lagged logistic, small values of rτ give a smooth

00:03:20.878 --> 00:03:24.800
approach to K. Intermediate values give damped oscillations.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.734
When rτ exceeds π/2, about 1.57,

00:03:27.734 --> 00:03:33.830
the population settles into a stable limit cycle and oscillates indefinitely.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:38.080
Populations with discrete generations can do stranger things still.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.728
In the logistic map, studied by Robert May in 1976,

00:03:41.728 --> 00:03:45.930
raising the growth rate drives the population from a stable point,

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:50.770
through a cascade of period-doubling bifurcations, into deterministic chaos.

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:58.571
Chaos means erratic, aperiodic fluctuations generated by a simple deterministic rule,

00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:01.640
with extreme sensitivity to initial conditions.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:06.550
May’s lesson: complex dynamics in nature need not have complex causes.

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:11.550
Real populations have structure.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:17.582
A life table tracks survivorship, lₓ, the proportion of a cohort alive at age x,

00:04:17.582 --> 00:04:22.440
and fecundity, mₓ, the offspring produced per individual at that age.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.740
Survivorship curves come in three classic types.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:30.630
Type I: most individuals survive to old age, as in large mammals.

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:35.180
Type II: a constant risk of death at every age, as in many birds.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:40.880
Type III: massive early mortality, as in oysters, most fish, and trees.

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:46.305
Summing lₓmₓ across ages gives R₀, the net reproductive rate:

00:04:46.305 --> 00:04:49.600
expected lifetime offspring per individual.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.960
When R₀ > 1, the population grows each generation.

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:57.010
Matrix models generalize the life table.

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:01.502
In a Leslie matrix, organized by age, or a Lefkovitch matrix,

00:05:01.502 --> 00:05:06.600
organized by stage, the dominant eigenvalue is the asymptotic growth rate λ.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:10.046
The right eigenvector is the stable stage distribution,

00:05:10.046 --> 00:05:12.870
and the left eigenvector gives reproductive values.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:18.290
Sensitivity and elasticity analyses ask which vital rate most affects λ.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:23.063
For loggerhead sea turtles, Crouse and colleagues showed in 1987 that λ responds

00:05:23.063 --> 00:05:26.616
far more to the survival of large juveniles than to egg survival,

00:05:26.616 --> 00:05:31.590
shifting conservation from nesting beaches toward turtle excluder devices on shrimp trawls.

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:36.380
Finally, chance.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:41.452
Demographic stochasticity is randomness in the fates of individuals:

00:05:41.452 --> 00:05:43.650
who lives, who dies, who breeds.

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:46.210
It matters most in small populations.

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:50.488
Environmental stochasticity is year-to-year variation in vital rates

00:05:50.488 --> 00:05:54.880
that affects all individuals together, and catastrophes are its extreme form.

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:57.500
Under environmental variation,

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:02.050
long-run growth follows the geometric mean of λ, not the arithmetic mean.

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:08.327
Because the geometric mean is always lower, variability itself depresses growth:

00:06:08.327 --> 00:06:12.000
a population averaging λ = 1.05 can still decline.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:17.755
Population viability analysis projects these processes forward to estimate extinction risk,

00:06:17.755 --> 00:06:21.660
often as the probability of falling below a quasi-extinction threshold.

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:27.136
A minimum viable population is the smallest size with an acceptable chance of persistence;

00:06:27.136 --> 00:06:31.310
Shaffer’s classic benchmark was ninety-nine percent over a thousand years.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:36.980
Small populations can spiral downward as inbreeding, lost genetic variation,

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:40.540
Allee effects, and stochasticity reinforce one another.

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:44.770
Gilpin and Soulé called this the extinction vortex in 1986.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:47.090
Life history ties it together.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:53.924
MacArthur and Wilson’s r- and K-selection contrasted fast, fecund species favored in uncrowded,

00:06:53.924 --> 00:06:59.680
unpredictable settings with slow, competitive species favored near carrying capacity.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:04.749
Today the fast–slow continuum and Grime’s C-S-R triangle of competitors,

00:07:04.749 --> 00:07:08.250
stress-tolerators, and ruderals refine that dichotomy.

00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:12.190
Chapter two.

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:16.530
Niche theory: what a species needs, and what it does.

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:22.690
The niche is ecology’s most used, and most argued-over, concept.

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:27.739
Joseph Grinnell, in 1917, framed it as a species’ habitat requirements:

00:07:27.739 --> 00:07:29.900
the conditions where it can live.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:33.334
The niche of his California thrasher was the chaparral

00:07:33.334 --> 00:07:36.030
that met its needs for food, cover, and nesting.

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:40.496
Charles Elton, in 1927, emphasized function instead:

00:07:40.496 --> 00:07:46.070
a species’ place in the biotic environment, its relations to food and enemies.

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:52.170
As Eugene Odum later put it, habitat is a species’ address, and the niche is its profession.

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:57.680
G. Evelyn Hutchinson, in 1957, made the niche quantitative.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:03.200
Treat each environmental factor, such as temperature, moisture, or food size, as an axis.

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:06.929
The set of conditions under which a species can persist

00:08:06.929 --> 00:08:10.560
forms an n-dimensional hypervolume: the Hutchinsonian niche.

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:12.230
Notice the shift.

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:15.095
Grinnell and Elton described niches as places

00:08:15.095 --> 00:08:18.820
or roles in the environment and the community, waiting to be filled.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:22.060
Hutchinson made the niche a property of the species itself.

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:28.686
Hutchinson distinguished the fundamental niche, the full range of conditions

00:08:28.686 --> 00:08:33.857
where a species could persist on its physiological tolerances alone, from the realized niche,

00:08:33.857 --> 00:08:39.140
the narrower range it actually occupies once competitors, predators, and pathogens are present.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:43.171
Joseph Connell’s experiments on the Isle of Cumbrae,

00:08:43.171 --> 00:08:46.200
in Scotland, published in 1961, made this vivid.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:48.540
Two barnacles share the rocky shore.

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:51.584
Chthamalus lives high in the intertidal zone,

00:08:51.584 --> 00:08:54.640
and Balanus, now called Semibalanus, lives below it.

00:08:55.390 --> 00:08:59.970
When Connell removed Balanus, Chthamalus survived and thrived far lower on the shore.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:02.325
Its fundamental niche extended deep,

00:09:02.325 --> 00:09:06.290
but there the faster-growing Balanus smothered, undercut, and crushed it.

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:10.480
Meanwhile, desiccation at low tide set the upper limit of Balanus.

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:13.130
This yields a widely observed rule.

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:17.983
Physical stress tends to set a species’ range limit on the harsh side of a gradient,

00:09:17.983 --> 00:09:21.110
while biotic interactions set the limit on the benign side.

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:30.290
Hutchinson’s 1959 essay, Homage to Santa Rosalia, asked why there are so many kinds of animals.

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:32.470
One answer is niche partitioning.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:37.542
In 1958, Robert MacArthur showed that five species of warbler coexist in the same

00:09:37.542 --> 00:09:42.040
spruce forests by foraging in different parts of the trees, in different ways.

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:47.780
Picture each species as a resource utilization curve along an axis such as prey size.

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:53.900
Each curve has a width, w, and neighboring curves are separated by a distance, d.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:57.500
The overlap between curves measures the potential for competition.

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:02.388
MacArthur and Levins argued in 1967 for limiting similarity:

00:10:02.388 --> 00:10:08.180
a maximum overlap compatible with coexistence, reached roughly when d falls below w.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:12.336
Hutchinson had noticed that coexisting congeners often differ in body

00:10:12.336 --> 00:10:17.290
or bill size by a ratio of about 1.3, though that pattern proved weaker than first thought.

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:23.690
Where competitors meet, natural selection can push their traits apart: character displacement.

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:30.160
In the Galápagos, the ground finches Geospiza fortis and G. fuliginosa have distinct beak depths

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:35.850
where they co-occur, but converge on intermediate beaks on islands where each lives alone.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:41.399
Present-day partitioning may therefore be the ghost of competition past,

00:10:41.399 --> 00:10:45.620
Connell’s phrase for patterns shaped by competition that no longer operates.

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:49.320
A lack of competition today does not mean competition never mattered.

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:57.040
In 2003, Jonathan Chase and Mathew Leibold reunited the Grinnellian and Eltonian traditions.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:02.459
A species’ niche has two halves: its requirements, the conditions under which its per-capita

00:11:02.459 --> 00:11:07.150
growth rate is at least zero, and its impacts, the effects it has on those conditions.

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:14.530
On a graph of two resources, requirements become a zero net growth isocline, or ZNGI.

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:18.690
Above the line the population grows; below it, it shrinks.

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:23.720
Impacts become consumption vectors, showing how the species depletes each resource.

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:29.330
Tilman’s resource competition theory, coming next, is built from exactly these pieces.

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:33.635
The Grinnellian niche lives on in species distribution models,

00:11:33.635 --> 00:11:37.820
which relate occurrences to environmental layers to map suitable habitat.

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:42.693
Soberón and Peterson’s BAM diagram clarifies what such models estimate:

00:11:42.693 --> 00:11:45.907
a species occurs where suitable abiotic conditions,

00:11:45.907 --> 00:11:50.380
favorable biotic interactions, and access through movement all overlap.

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:53.780
Finally, organisms reshape their own environments.

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:56.940
Beavers flood valleys; earthworms remake soils.

00:11:57.340 --> 00:11:59.772
This niche construction can alter natural selection

00:11:59.772 --> 00:12:02.060
on the constructors and on everyone around them.

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:05.820
Chapter three.

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:10.760
Competition: what happens when organisms need the same limited resources.

00:12:13.460 --> 00:12:16.947
Competition is a mutually negative interaction, minus–minus,

00:12:16.947 --> 00:12:20.260
between organisms that require the same limited resource.

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:26.040
Intraspecific competition occurs among members of one species and drives density dependence.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:29.330
Interspecific competition occurs between species.

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:33.509
By mechanism, exploitative competition is indirect:

00:12:33.509 --> 00:12:38.350
individuals deplete a shared resource, such as water, nitrogen, or prey.

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:44.437
Interference competition is direct: individuals prevent others from using a resource,

00:12:44.437 --> 00:12:48.050
through aggression, territoriality, or chemical means.

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:54.376
Thomas Schoener, in 1983, listed six mechanisms: consumptive competition for resources;

00:12:54.376 --> 00:13:00.143
preemptive, occupying space first; overgrowth, as when plants or corals shade one another;

00:13:00.143 --> 00:13:05.334
chemical, or allelopathy, like the juglone released by black walnut; territorial;

00:13:05.334 --> 00:13:08.090
and encounter, such as fights at a carcass.

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:14.331
A. J. Nicholson contrasted scramble competition, where resources are divided

00:13:14.331 --> 00:13:19.677
so evenly that every individual gets too little, with contest competition,

00:13:19.677 --> 00:13:23.940
where winners secure what they need and losers get nothing.

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:30.088
Competition is also often asymmetric: in plants, competition for light is size-asymmetric,

00:13:30.088 --> 00:13:33.920
because taller plants shade shorter ones disproportionately.

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:39.310
And beware apparent competition, described by Robert Holt in 1977.

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:42.729
Two prey species that never share a resource can still

00:13:42.729 --> 00:13:45.580
depress each other by sustaining a shared predator.

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:50.750
In field data it looks just like competition, but it is mediated by a natural enemy.

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:57.130
The Lotka–Volterra competition model extends the logistic.

00:13:57.530 --> 00:14:02.481
Each species’ growth is slowed by its own density and by the density of its competitor,

00:14:02.481 --> 00:14:04.530
scaled by a competition coefficient.

00:14:04.930 --> 00:14:08.923
α₁₂ is the per-capita effect of species two on species one,

00:14:08.923 --> 00:14:12.780
measured relative to the effect of species one on itself.

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:15.490
Plot both species in a phase plane.

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:18.176
Each species has a zero-growth isocline,

00:14:18.176 --> 00:14:22.120
a straight line along which its population neither grows nor shrinks.

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:26.076
How the two lines sit relative to each other determines the outcome,

00:14:26.076 --> 00:14:28.220
and there are exactly four possibilities.

00:14:28.970 --> 00:14:34.300
If species one’s isocline lies entirely outside species two’s, species one always wins.

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:36.950
Reverse them, and species two wins.

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:39.390
Either way, the loser is excluded.

00:14:39.790 --> 00:14:43.709
Georgii Gause saw this in his Paramecium cultures in the 1930s,

00:14:43.709 --> 00:14:49.120
which gave us the competitive exclusion principle: complete competitors cannot coexist.

00:14:50.370 --> 00:14:56.207
If the isoclines cross, and each species limits itself more than it limits its competitor,

00:14:56.207 --> 00:14:59.450
the crossing is a stable equilibrium: coexistence.

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:05.010
If instead each limits its competitor more than itself, the crossing is an unstable saddle.

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:11.000
One species still wins, but which one depends on who starts ahead, a classic priority effect.

00:15:12.250 --> 00:15:16.077
The rule to remember: stable coexistence requires intraspecific

00:15:16.077 --> 00:15:19.600
competition to be stronger than interspecific competition.

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:22.980
Species must limit themselves more than they limit each other.

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:30.680
Lotka–Volterra describes the outcome of competition, but not its mechanism.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:34.870
David Tilman’s resource competition theory makes the mechanism explicit.

00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:38.584
For a single limiting resource, each species has an R*,

00:15:38.584 --> 00:15:42.620
the resource level at which its growth exactly balances its losses.

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:46.980
A consumer draws the resource down until it reaches its own R*.

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:52.178
So when species compete for one resource, the species with the lowest R* wins:

00:15:52.178 --> 00:15:56.730
it depresses the resource below the level its competitor needs to persist.

00:15:57.130 --> 00:16:02.050
This R* rule has held up in experiments with algae, bacteria, and grassland plants.

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:08.119
With two essential resources, each species’ zero net growth isocline is L-shaped,

00:16:08.119 --> 00:16:10.320
and two species’ isoclines can cross.

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:14.994
They coexist stably when each species is more limited by a different resource,

00:16:14.994 --> 00:16:18.830
and each consumes relatively more of the resource that limits it most.

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:22.149
Which outcome occurs depends on the supply point,

00:16:22.149 --> 00:16:24.770
the rates at which the two resources are supplied.

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:29.974
That is Tilman’s resource-ratio hypothesis: shifting the ratio of nutrients,

00:16:29.974 --> 00:16:33.830
by fertilization for instance, can reorder which species win.

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:42.560
Peter Chesson’s modern coexistence theory, published in 2000, reorganized all of this.

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:45.874
Its test for coexistence is mutual invasibility:

00:16:45.874 --> 00:16:51.520
each species must be able to increase when rare, invading a community dominated by the other.

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:54.090
Mechanisms come in two kinds.

00:16:54.490 --> 00:17:00.032
Stabilizing mechanisms, or niche differences, cause species to limit themselves more than each other,

00:17:00.032 --> 00:17:03.050
so any species gains an advantage when it becomes rare.

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:06.694
Equalizing mechanisms shrink average fitness differences,

00:17:06.694 --> 00:17:10.280
the inherent competitive advantage of one species over another.

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:14.770
Species coexist when niche differences overcome fitness differences.

00:17:15.170 --> 00:17:19.400
On this plane, the zone of coexistence widens as niche differences grow.

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:23.436
Equalizing mechanisms alone can never produce stable coexistence,

00:17:23.436 --> 00:17:26.010
but they reduce the niche difference required.

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:31.782
Stabilizing mechanisms include resource partitioning and Janzen–Connell effects,

00:17:31.782 --> 00:17:35.570
where specialist natural enemies kill seedlings near their parents.

00:17:35.970 --> 00:17:37.710
Others depend on fluctuations.

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:42.795
The storage effect operates when species respond differently to a variable environment

00:17:42.795 --> 00:17:46.880
and can store gains, in seed banks or long-lived adults, through bad years.

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:52.790
Relative nonlinearity operates when species respond nonlinearly to fluctuating resources.

00:17:53.540 --> 00:17:57.906
Together these ideas resolve Hutchinson’s 1961 paradox of the plankton:

00:17:57.906 --> 00:18:04.240
how dozens of phytoplankton species coexist on a handful of resources in an apparently well-mixed lake.

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:06.714
Variation in time, natural enemies,

00:18:06.714 --> 00:18:11.100
and hidden spatial structure keep the system from ever reaching exclusion.

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:15.000
Chapter four.

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:18.290
Predation: the dynamics of eating and being eaten.

00:18:20.990 --> 00:18:25.931
Predation is a plus–minus interaction, and the term spans true predators,

00:18:25.931 --> 00:18:28.570
herbivores, parasitoids, and parasites.

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:32.271
Its simplest model, from Lotka and Volterra in the 1920s,

00:18:32.271 --> 00:18:35.630
gives prey exponential growth in the absence of predators,

00:18:35.630 --> 00:18:40.900
and lets predators convert captured prey into new predators while dying at a constant rate.

00:18:41.650 --> 00:18:44.010
The model’s signature is coupled oscillation.

00:18:44.410 --> 00:18:49.072
Prey increase, then predators increase; prey crash, and predators follow,

00:18:49.072 --> 00:18:53.160
with predator peaks lagging prey peaks by about a quarter cycle.

00:18:53.910 --> 00:18:58.200
In the phase plane, the two populations circle their equilibrium in closed loops.

00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:02.762
These neutral cycles have an amplitude set entirely by the starting conditions,

00:19:02.762 --> 00:19:05.660
and any disturbance shifts the system onto a new cycle.

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:10.320
The model is structurally unstable, better for intuition than for prediction.

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:12.550
Yet real cycles exist.

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:14.824
Snowshoe hares and Canada lynx,

00:19:14.824 --> 00:19:19.420
recorded in Hudson’s Bay Company fur returns, cycle roughly every ten years.

00:19:19.820 --> 00:19:23.180
Experiments by Charles Krebs and colleagues in the Yukon showed

00:19:23.180 --> 00:19:26.753
that the hare cycle arises from food and predation acting together,

00:19:26.753 --> 00:19:30.700
with predation risk also causing stress that suppresses hare reproduction.

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:39.200
In 1959, C. S. Holling asked how a predator’s feeding rate changes with prey density.

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:43.120
The answer is its functional response, and it comes in three types.

00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:49.620
A Type I response rises linearly until the predator is satiated, typical of filter feeders.

00:19:50.020 --> 00:19:54.120
A Type II response decelerates, because each capture costs handling time.

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:58.082
Holling’s disc equation gives intake as aN / (1 + ahN),

00:19:58.082 --> 00:20:01.580
where a is the attack rate and h is the handling time.

00:20:01.980 --> 00:20:04.060
Intake saturates at 1/h.

00:20:04.810 --> 00:20:10.085
A Type III response is sigmoid: predators take few prey at low density, then accelerate,

00:20:10.085 --> 00:20:16.020
through learning a search image, switching between prey types, or because scarce prey find refuges.

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:18.380
The type matters for stability.

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:22.874
With Type II, per-capita risk to prey falls as prey become more numerous,

00:20:22.874 --> 00:20:25.790
an inverse density dependence that is destabilizing.

00:20:26.190 --> 00:20:29.914
With Type III, risk rises with prey density at low densities,

00:20:29.914 --> 00:20:33.210
which can regulate prey and stabilize the interaction.

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:36.928
The numerical response is the change in predator numbers,

00:20:36.928 --> 00:20:39.740
through reproduction or immigration, as prey increase.

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:44.499
Multiply the functional and numerical responses to get the total response:

00:20:44.499 --> 00:20:47.680
the overall predation pressure on the prey population.

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:53.430
From the predator’s side, these choices are decisions.

00:20:53.830 --> 00:20:57.445
Optimal foraging theory assumes that natural selection favors foragers

00:20:57.445 --> 00:20:59.820
that maximize their net rate of energy intake.

00:21:00.570 --> 00:21:04.293
In the classic diet model, prey types are ranked by profitability:

00:21:04.293 --> 00:21:06.550
energy gained per unit of handling time.

00:21:06.950 --> 00:21:09.793
A forager should always take the most profitable prey,

00:21:09.793 --> 00:21:12.900
and add lower-ranked prey only when better prey are scarce.

00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:16.679
Whether to eat a poor item depends on the abundance of good items,

00:21:16.679 --> 00:21:18.420
not on the abundance of poor ones.

00:21:19.170 --> 00:21:23.650
For patchy food, Eric Charnov’s marginal value theorem predicts when to leave.

00:21:24.050 --> 00:21:28.970
Gains within a patch diminish as it is depleted, so a forager should leave when its instantaneous

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:33.180
intake rate falls to the average rate for the whole habitat, including travel time.

00:21:33.580 --> 00:21:37.400
The longer the travel between patches, the longer a forager should stay in each.

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:45.867
Add two realistic ingredients, prey self-limitation and a Type II functional response,

00:21:45.867 --> 00:21:48.460
and you have the Rosenzweig–MacArthur model.

00:21:48.860 --> 00:21:50.610
The prey isocline becomes a hump.

00:21:51.010 --> 00:21:54.028
The predator isocline is a vertical line at the prey density

00:21:54.028 --> 00:21:56.040
where predators just replace themselves.

00:21:56.790 --> 00:22:00.009
If the predator isocline crosses to the right of the hump,

00:22:00.009 --> 00:22:02.840
trajectories spiral inward to a stable equilibrium.

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:07.430
If it crosses to the left, they spiral outward onto a stable limit cycle.

00:22:08.180 --> 00:22:11.530
Now enrich the system by raising the prey’s carrying capacity.

00:22:11.930 --> 00:22:14.658
The hump shifts right, past the predator isocline,

00:22:14.658 --> 00:22:20.170
and stable coexistence gives way to ever-larger cycles that can drive both species toward extinction.

00:22:20.570 --> 00:22:24.540
Michael Rosenzweig called this the paradox of enrichment in 1971.

00:22:26.090 --> 00:22:28.794
Host–parasitoid systems have their own classic,

00:22:28.794 --> 00:22:32.590
the Nicholson–Bailey model, whose oscillations grow without bound.

00:22:32.990 --> 00:22:38.142
Persistence returns with aggregated attacks, prey refuges, or spatial structure,

00:22:38.142 --> 00:22:42.007
echoing Carl Huffaker’s 1958 experiments, in which predatory

00:22:42.007 --> 00:22:47.030
and herbivorous mites persisted only in a complex, patchy universe of oranges.

00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:52.690
Predators can structure entire communities.

00:22:53.090 --> 00:22:58.540
In 1966, Robert Paine removed the sea star Pisaster from rocky shores in Washington.

00:22:58.940 --> 00:23:01.091
Without it, mussels monopolized the space

00:23:01.091 --> 00:23:04.030
and the community dropped from fifteen species to eight.

00:23:04.430 --> 00:23:06.463
Paine called such species keystones:

00:23:06.463 --> 00:23:10.360
their impact is disproportionately large relative to their abundance.

00:23:11.110 --> 00:23:13.170
Effects can cascade down food chains.

00:23:13.570 --> 00:23:17.834
Hairston, Smith, and Slobodkin’s 1960 green world hypothesis argued

00:23:17.834 --> 00:23:21.970
that predators hold herbivores in check, keeping the world green.

00:23:22.370 --> 00:23:26.510
In the Aleutian Islands, sea otters eat sea urchins, which graze kelp.

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:32.540
When killer whales began eating otters in the 1990s, urchins boomed and kelp forests collapsed.

00:23:33.290 --> 00:23:37.663
This alternating pattern of effects down a food chain is a trophic cascade,

00:23:37.663 --> 00:23:42.910
a form of top-down control, as opposed to bottom-up control by resources and productivity.

00:23:43.310 --> 00:23:48.329
When top predators disappear, smaller predators can surge: mesopredator release,

00:23:48.329 --> 00:23:55.230
as when the loss of coyotes from fragmented scrub let foxes and house cats increase, and scrub birds declined.

00:23:55.980 --> 00:23:58.020
Predators also matter without killing.

00:23:58.420 --> 00:24:04.270
Prey alter foraging, vigilance, habitat use, and physiology in response to risk.

00:24:04.670 --> 00:24:07.660
These non-consumptive effects make up the ecology of fear.

00:24:08.060 --> 00:24:12.405
In a 2011 experiment, Liana Zanette and colleagues found that broadcasting

00:24:12.405 --> 00:24:17.220
predator calls alone cut song sparrow offspring production by about forty percent.

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:21.100
Spatial variation in perceived risk forms a landscape of fear.

00:24:24.300 --> 00:24:25.040
Chapter five.

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:30.440
Island biogeography: species richness as a balance of arrivals and losses.

00:24:33.140 --> 00:24:38.090
Islands are natural experiments: replicated, bounded, and easy to count.

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:43.670
One of ecology’s oldest generalizations comes from them, the species–area relationship.

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:46.090
Larger areas hold more species.

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:51.480
It is usually written as a power law, S = cA^z.

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:58.690
On log–log axes this becomes a straight line, log S = log c + z log A, with slope z.

00:24:59.440 --> 00:25:04.300
For true islands, z usually falls between about 0.2 and 0.35.

00:25:04.700 --> 00:25:09.840
For nested samples of a contiguous mainland it is lower, roughly 0.12 to 0.17,

00:25:09.840 --> 00:25:16.430
because small mainland samples include transient species spilling over from the surrounding habitat.

00:25:17.180 --> 00:25:21.905
With z near 0.3, a tenfold loss of area roughly halves the number of species,

00:25:21.905 --> 00:25:24.850
a rule of thumb attributed to Philip Darlington.

00:25:25.250 --> 00:25:30.500
The same arithmetic, run in reverse, underlies many estimates of extinction from habitat loss.

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:37.573
In 1963 and 1967, Robert MacArthur and E. O. Wilson

00:25:37.573 --> 00:25:41.750
proposed the equilibrium theory of island biogeography.

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:46.504
The number of species on an island reflects a dynamic balance between two rates:

00:25:46.504 --> 00:25:50.150
the immigration of new species and the extinction of resident ones.

00:25:50.900 --> 00:25:53.509
The immigration rate falls as an island fills,

00:25:53.509 --> 00:25:57.650
reaching zero when all P species of the mainland source pool are present.

00:25:58.050 --> 00:26:00.461
The extinction rate rises with richness,

00:26:00.461 --> 00:26:04.800
because more species means smaller populations and more species to lose.

00:26:05.200 --> 00:26:08.557
Both curves bend: the best dispersers arrive first,

00:26:08.557 --> 00:26:11.650
and competition intensifies as species pack in.

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:17.290
Where the curves cross lies the equilibrium species richness, Ŝ.

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:20.319
The rate at that crossing is the turnover rate:

00:26:20.319 --> 00:26:24.570
even when richness holds steady, the identity of the species keeps changing.

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:27.769
Distance controls immigration:

00:26:27.769 --> 00:26:32.380
near islands receive more colonists than far ones, the distance effect.

00:26:32.780 --> 00:26:34.494
Area controls extinction:

00:26:34.494 --> 00:26:39.840
large islands support larger populations that persist longer, the area effect.

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:45.180
So large, near islands hold the most species, and small, far islands the fewest.

00:26:48.580 --> 00:26:50.160
The theory was tested directly.

00:26:50.560 --> 00:26:54.345
Daniel Simberloff and E. O. Wilson tented small mangrove islands

00:26:54.345 --> 00:26:58.190
in the Florida Keys and fumigated them to remove every arthropod.

00:26:58.590 --> 00:27:02.805
Within about a year, species numbers returned close to their original levels,

00:27:02.805 --> 00:27:06.910
with the nearest island richest, but the species were often different ones.

00:27:07.310 --> 00:27:10.490
That is equilibrium with turnover, as predicted.

00:27:11.240 --> 00:27:12.380
Refinements followed.

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:18.104
James Brown and Astrid Kodric-Brown described the rescue effect in 1977:

00:27:18.104 --> 00:27:24.390
on near islands, immigrants bolster dwindling populations, lowering extinction rates.

00:27:24.790 --> 00:27:27.169
The target effect works the other way:

00:27:27.169 --> 00:27:31.050
larger islands intercept more dispersers, raising immigration.

00:27:31.450 --> 00:27:35.810
Both blur the neat assignment of distance to immigration and area to extinction.

00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:39.120
Real islands also add time and evolution.

00:27:39.520 --> 00:27:44.980
Krakatau, sterilized by its 1883 eruption, has been reassembling ever since.

00:27:45.380 --> 00:27:50.086
And on the most remote archipelagos, like Hawaiʻi, speciation within the islands,

00:27:50.086 --> 00:27:53.340
not immigration, becomes the main source of new species.

00:27:56.740 --> 00:27:59.592
The theory’s greatest influence was on conservation,

00:27:59.592 --> 00:28:03.650
because fragments of habitat behave like islands in a sea of altered land.

00:28:04.050 --> 00:28:07.406
A newly isolated fragment holds more species than its new,

00:28:07.406 --> 00:28:11.340
smaller equilibrium, so its richness decays toward that lower level.

00:28:11.740 --> 00:28:15.380
This decay is called relaxation, or faunal collapse.

00:28:16.130 --> 00:28:20.520
The species destined to vanish but not yet gone constitute an extinction debt,

00:28:20.520 --> 00:28:24.290
a debt that will be paid unless habitat is restored or reconnected.

00:28:24.690 --> 00:28:29.147
William Newmark found in 1987 that western North American national parks had lost

00:28:29.147 --> 00:28:33.660
mammal species since their establishment, with the smallest parks losing the most.

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:38.415
Island theory also sparked the SLOSS debate: for the same total area,

00:28:38.415 --> 00:28:42.420
is it better to protect a single large reserve or several small ones?

00:28:42.820 --> 00:28:47.438
A single large reserve supports area-sensitive species and larger populations;

00:28:47.438 --> 00:28:51.700
several small reserves can capture more habitat variety and spread risk.

00:28:52.100 --> 00:28:55.958
The answer depends on nestedness, dispersal, and the threats involved,

00:28:55.958 --> 00:28:59.100
and the question led directly to metapopulation thinking.

00:29:02.300 --> 00:29:03.090
Chapter six.

00:29:03.490 --> 00:29:07.610
Metapopulations: persistence across a network of habitat patches.

00:29:10.310 --> 00:29:16.978
A metapopulation is a population of populations: a set of local populations in discrete habitat patches,

00:29:16.978 --> 00:29:21.210
connected by dispersal, each with its own chance of going extinct.

00:29:21.610 --> 00:29:27.600
Richard Levins coined the term in 1969, while thinking about the control of agricultural pests.

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:32.970
Levins tracked not individuals but the fraction of patches occupied, p.

00:29:33.370 --> 00:29:41.670
Empty patches are colonized at rate c·p·(1 − p), and occupied patches go extinct at rate e·p.

00:29:42.070 --> 00:29:47.240
So dp/dt = cp(1 − p) − ep.

00:29:47.990 --> 00:29:51.698
This has the same form as the logistic equation,

00:29:51.698 --> 00:29:55.870
and occupancy settles at an equilibrium, p* = 1 − e/c.

00:29:57.420 --> 00:30:04.322
The deep insight: a metapopulation can persist indefinitely even though every local population

00:30:04.322 --> 00:30:10.930
eventually blinks out, provided colonization outpaces extinction, that is, provided c > e.

00:30:11.330 --> 00:30:15.030
Persistence is a property of the network, not of any single patch.

00:30:18.430 --> 00:30:20.590
Now destroy a fraction D of the patches.

00:30:20.990 --> 00:30:26.610
Equilibrium occupancy becomes p* = 1 − D − e/c.

00:30:27.010 --> 00:30:30.220
Occupancy falls one-for-one with habitat destroyed.

00:30:31.470 --> 00:30:37.920
The metapopulation goes extinct when D reaches 1 − e/c, while suitable habitat still remains.

00:30:38.320 --> 00:30:40.216
This is the extinction threshold:

00:30:40.216 --> 00:30:45.100
a species can disappear from a landscape that still contains empty, suitable patches.

00:30:46.850 --> 00:30:47.890
Two lessons follow.

00:30:48.290 --> 00:30:51.509
Empty patches are not evidence that habitat is unimportant;

00:30:51.509 --> 00:30:55.110
they may be the very patches the network needs for recolonization.

00:30:55.510 --> 00:30:58.571
And because decline toward a new equilibrium takes time,

00:30:58.571 --> 00:31:02.890
species that persist after habitat loss may already be committed to extinction.

00:31:03.290 --> 00:31:07.092
Tilman and colleagues argued in 1994 that the best competitors,

00:31:07.092 --> 00:31:10.110
often the poorest colonizers, are the first to go.

00:31:13.510 --> 00:31:15.910
Not every metapopulation looks like Levins’s.

00:31:16.310 --> 00:31:18.549
In a mainland–island system, one large,

00:31:18.549 --> 00:31:23.370
effectively permanent population supplies colonists to small, extinction-prone ones.

00:31:23.770 --> 00:31:29.180
In a patchy population, dispersal is so frequent that the patches behave as a single population.

00:31:29.580 --> 00:31:34.416
In a nonequilibrium metapopulation, extinctions outpace recolonization,

00:31:34.416 --> 00:31:38.230
often after fragmentation, and the system is in decline.

00:31:38.980 --> 00:31:43.060
Ronald Pulliam’s 1988 source–sink model adds habitat quality.

00:31:43.460 --> 00:31:49.670
In source habitats, births exceed deaths, λ > 1, and surplus individuals emigrate.

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:55.330
In sink habitats, λ < 1, and populations persist only through immigration.

00:31:56.080 --> 00:31:58.565
A large share of a species can live in sinks,

00:31:58.565 --> 00:32:02.100
so local abundance can be a misleading guide to habitat quality.

00:32:02.500 --> 00:32:06.272
Worse is an ecological trap: a sink that animals actively prefer,

00:32:06.272 --> 00:32:09.638
because cues that once signaled good habitat no longer do,

00:32:09.638 --> 00:32:13.990
as when grassland birds nest in hayfields mowed before their chicks fledge.

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:21.210
Ilkka Hanski made the theory spatially realistic.

00:32:21.610 --> 00:32:26.643
His incidence function model predicts each patch’s probability of occupancy from its area,

00:32:26.643 --> 00:32:29.383
which sets extinction risk, and its connectivity,

00:32:29.383 --> 00:32:33.410
a sum of contributions from occupied patches weighted by their distance.

00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:38.907
His test case became the flagship of the field: the Glanville fritillary butterfly,

00:32:38.907 --> 00:32:44.170
living as a metapopulation across some four thousand dry meadows in Finland’s Åland Islands,

00:32:44.170 --> 00:32:47.430
where local populations wink out and reappear every year.

00:32:48.180 --> 00:32:50.010
Two more forces shape persistence.

00:32:50.410 --> 00:32:54.340
Dispersal provides rescue, but it can also synchronize local dynamics,

00:32:54.340 --> 00:32:56.810
and so can shared weather, the Moran effect.

00:32:57.210 --> 00:33:00.194
When patches fluctuate in synchrony they crash together,

00:33:00.194 --> 00:33:03.870
and the spreading of risk that lets a metapopulation persist is lost.

00:33:07.070 --> 00:33:07.890
Chapter seven.

00:33:08.290 --> 00:33:11.740
Metacommunities: linking local communities across a region.

00:33:14.440 --> 00:33:18.592
A metacommunity, as defined by Mathew Leibold and colleagues in 2004,

00:33:18.592 --> 00:33:24.550
is a set of local communities linked by the dispersal of multiple, potentially interacting species.

00:33:24.950 --> 00:33:27.970
It is the community-level counterpart of the metapopulation.

00:33:28.720 --> 00:33:31.260
Its diversity is partitioned across scales.

00:33:31.660 --> 00:33:36.885
Alpha diversity is local richness; gamma diversity is the regional total;

00:33:36.885 --> 00:33:42.110
and beta diversity, the variation in composition among sites, links them.

00:33:42.510 --> 00:33:46.440
In Whittaker’s multiplicative form, γ = α × β.

00:33:47.690 --> 00:33:54.096
Beta diversity itself has two components: turnover, as species replace one another along gradients,

00:33:54.096 --> 00:33:59.790
and nestedness, as species-poor sites hold subsets of the species found at richer sites.

00:34:01.040 --> 00:34:06.580
Community assembly is often pictured as a series of filters acting on a regional species pool.

00:34:06.980 --> 00:34:12.738
Dispersal determines who arrives, environmental filtering determines who can tolerate local conditions,

00:34:12.738 --> 00:34:15.310
and biotic interactions determine who remains.

00:34:15.710 --> 00:34:18.867
Kraft and colleagues cautioned in 2015 that competition can

00:34:18.867 --> 00:34:22.130
produce patterns easily mistaken for environmental filtering.

00:34:25.530 --> 00:34:28.210
Leibold and colleagues described four paradigms.

00:34:28.610 --> 00:34:33.219
In patch dynamics, patches are identical, and species coexist regionally

00:34:33.219 --> 00:34:38.789
through a competition–colonization trade-off: superior competitors are poor dispersers,

00:34:38.789 --> 00:34:42.630
so fugitive species persist by reaching empty patches first.

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:46.909
In species sorting, patches differ in environmental conditions,

00:34:46.909 --> 00:34:49.430
and each species does best in different ones.

00:34:49.830 --> 00:34:53.322
Dispersal is sufficient for species to reach suitable patches,

00:34:53.322 --> 00:34:55.800
so local composition tracks the environment.

00:34:56.200 --> 00:34:58.900
This is classic niche theory, set in space.

00:34:59.650 --> 00:35:03.620
In mass effects, dispersal is so high that it overrides local fitness.

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:09.355
Immigrants spill from patches where a species thrives into patches where it cannot persist alone,

00:35:09.355 --> 00:35:13.040
a community-wide source–sink dynamic that inflates local diversity.

00:35:13.440 --> 00:35:17.530
At extreme dispersal, the region homogenizes and diversity falls.

00:35:18.280 --> 00:35:22.105
And in the neutral paradigm, species are demographically equivalent,

00:35:22.105 --> 00:35:25.930
and composition drifts through chance births, deaths, and dispersal.

00:35:26.330 --> 00:35:28.250
We will return to it in the next chapter.

00:35:31.650 --> 00:35:35.030
Across these paradigms, dispersal rate is a master variable.

00:35:35.430 --> 00:35:39.641
Theory by Nicolas Mouquet and Michel Loreau predicts that local diversity

00:35:39.641 --> 00:35:43.910
peaks at intermediate dispersal, while beta diversity declines steadily as

00:35:43.910 --> 00:35:48.410
dispersal homogenizes the region, and regional diversity eventually falls too.

00:35:49.660 --> 00:35:53.303
To diagnose which processes structure a real metacommunity,

00:35:53.303 --> 00:35:57.872
ecologists often use variation partitioning: how much variation in species

00:35:57.872 --> 00:36:03.800
composition is explained by environment alone, by spatial structure alone, and by both together.

00:36:04.200 --> 00:36:07.564
A large environmental fraction suggests species sorting;

00:36:07.564 --> 00:36:11.710
a large pure spatial fraction suggests dispersal limitation or drift.

00:36:12.460 --> 00:36:14.990
Treat these fractions as clues, not verdicts.

00:36:15.390 --> 00:36:20.598
Unmeasured environmental variables that are spatially structured can masquerade as dispersal effects,

00:36:20.598 --> 00:36:24.310
and very different processes can leave similar statistical fingerprints.

00:36:27.510 --> 00:36:28.120
Chapter eight.

00:36:28.520 --> 00:36:32.430
Neutral theory: what chance and dispersal alone can explain.

00:36:35.130 --> 00:36:38.030
Niche theory explains coexistence through differences.

00:36:38.430 --> 00:36:42.397
In 2001, Stephen Hubbell’s Unified Neutral Theory of Biodiversity

00:36:42.397 --> 00:36:45.204
and Biogeography asked a provocative question:

00:36:45.204 --> 00:36:50.270
how much of nature’s pattern could we explain if species had no differences at all?

00:36:51.020 --> 00:36:56.230
Neutral theory assumes ecological equivalence: every individual of every species

00:36:56.230 --> 00:37:01.180
has identical per-capita chances of birth, death, dispersal, and speciation.

00:37:01.580 --> 00:37:05.040
It is a theory built on individuals, not on species traits.

00:37:05.790 --> 00:37:11.540
Communities are zero-sum: a fixed number of individuals, J, saturates the landscape.

00:37:11.940 --> 00:37:16.450
Whenever one dies, it is replaced by the offspring of another, chosen at random.

00:37:16.850 --> 00:37:22.014
Species abundances therefore wander by chance, a process called ecological drift,

00:37:22.014 --> 00:37:27.880
directly analogous to genetic drift in Motoo Kimura’s neutral theory of molecular evolution.

00:37:29.430 --> 00:37:35.950
In an isolated community, drift alone eventually leads to monodominance: one species takes over.

00:37:36.350 --> 00:37:40.996
Diversity persists only if it is replenished, by immigration from outside,

00:37:40.996 --> 00:37:44.010
and, over the longest timescales, by speciation.

00:37:47.910 --> 00:37:49.580
Hubbell’s model has two scales.

00:37:49.980 --> 00:37:55.350
In the metacommunity, new species arise by point speciation, at a rate ν per birth.

00:37:55.750 --> 00:38:02.507
Diversity there is governed by a single number, the fundamental biodiversity number θ = 2J_Mν,

00:38:02.507 --> 00:38:06.460
twice the metacommunity size times the speciation rate.

00:38:06.860 --> 00:38:13.000
At equilibrium, metacommunity abundances follow Fisher’s log-series, and θ equals Fisher’s α.

00:38:13.750 --> 00:38:16.050
Local communities draw from the metacommunity.

00:38:16.450 --> 00:38:21.963
With probability m, a dead individual is replaced by an immigrant from the metacommunity;

00:38:21.963 --> 00:38:25.060
otherwise, by the offspring of a local individual.

00:38:25.460 --> 00:38:28.864
The immigration probability m sets how strongly dispersal

00:38:28.864 --> 00:38:32.030
limitation separates local from regional composition.

00:38:32.780 --> 00:38:36.702
The theory’s central prediction is the species abundance distribution:

00:38:36.702 --> 00:38:39.280
how many species are common and how many rare.

00:38:39.680 --> 00:38:44.952
Frank Preston plotted abundances in doubling classes he called octaves, and found a humped,

00:38:44.952 --> 00:38:50.630
roughly lognormal shape, with the rarest species hidden behind a veil line of incomplete sampling.

00:38:51.380 --> 00:38:55.540
Hubbell’s zero-sum multinomial reproduces such humped distributions.

00:38:55.940 --> 00:39:01.444
Dispersal limitation leaves local communities with fewer rare species than the log-series predicts,

00:39:01.444 --> 00:39:03.890
because rare regional species seldom arrive.

00:39:07.290 --> 00:39:10.399
Neutral models fit the species abundance distribution of the

00:39:10.399 --> 00:39:13.870
fifty-hectare forest plot on Barro Colorado Island remarkably well.

00:39:14.270 --> 00:39:16.800
They also predict distance decay of similarity,

00:39:16.800 --> 00:39:21.000
as dispersal limitation makes nearby communities more alike than distant ones.

00:39:21.750 --> 00:39:24.370
But fitting a pattern is weak evidence for a process.

00:39:24.770 --> 00:39:28.472
Niche-based models can produce the same abundance distributions,

00:39:28.472 --> 00:39:31.943
and experiments routinely find stabilizing niche differences

00:39:31.943 --> 00:39:36.340
and species-specific negative density dependence, even among tropical trees.

00:39:36.740 --> 00:39:39.510
Strict neutrality is almost certainly false.

00:39:40.260 --> 00:39:44.300
Its lasting value is as a null model: neutral theory shows what chance

00:39:44.300 --> 00:39:48.860
and dispersal alone can produce, so that departures from it become informative.

00:39:49.260 --> 00:39:50.770
It also seeded a synthesis.

00:39:51.170 --> 00:39:54.330
In Chesson’s terms, neutrality is the special case of

00:39:54.330 --> 00:39:57.430
zero niche differences and zero fitness differences.

00:39:57.830 --> 00:40:01.322
And Scheffer and van Nes showed how emergent neutrality can arise,

00:40:01.322 --> 00:40:05.660
with clumps of similar species coexisting almost neutrally within distinct niches.

00:40:09.060 --> 00:40:09.800
Chapter nine.

00:40:10.200 --> 00:40:12.870
Synthesis: one framework for the whole toolkit.

00:40:15.570 --> 00:40:21.444
Mark Vellend’s theory of ecological communities, developed in 2010 and 2016,

00:40:21.444 --> 00:40:25.694
reduces community ecology to four high-level processes,

00:40:25.694 --> 00:40:31.800
borrowed from population genetics: selection, drift, dispersal, and speciation.

00:40:32.550 --> 00:40:37.759
Selection means deterministic fitness differences among species: niches, competition,

00:40:37.759 --> 00:40:42.600
predation, and the stabilizing and equalizing mechanisms of coexistence theory.

00:40:43.000 --> 00:40:48.180
Drift is demographic chance, decisive in small populations and the engine of neutral theory.

00:40:48.580 --> 00:40:50.992
Dispersal moves organisms among places,

00:40:50.992 --> 00:40:55.940
from island colonization to metapopulation rescue to metacommunity mass effects.

00:40:56.340 --> 00:40:59.110
Speciation adds new species to the regional pool.

00:40:59.860 --> 00:41:02.340
Every chapter you have seen is a particular blend.

00:41:02.740 --> 00:41:06.650
Island biogeography is dispersal set against stochastic extinction.

00:41:07.050 --> 00:41:10.800
Metapopulations are colonization and extinction among patches.

00:41:11.200 --> 00:41:13.920
Species sorting is selection plus dispersal.

00:41:14.320 --> 00:41:19.520
Neutral theory is drift, dispersal, and speciation, with selection set to zero.

00:41:23.420 --> 00:41:26.020
Before your exam, rehearse with questions like these.

00:41:26.420 --> 00:41:29.350
Why is a Type II functional response destabilizing?

00:41:29.750 --> 00:41:32.390
Why does the species with the lowest R* win?

00:41:32.790 --> 00:41:35.470
Why don’t empty patches mean habitat is unimportant?

00:41:37.720 --> 00:41:41.010
What separates a stabilizing mechanism from an equalizing one?

00:41:41.410 --> 00:41:46.550
How do the four metacommunity paradigms differ in dispersal and environmental heterogeneity?

00:41:46.950 --> 00:41:49.620
And what does the fundamental biodiversity number measure?

00:41:51.870 --> 00:41:55.159
If you can sketch the graphs from memory, the isoclines,

00:41:55.159 --> 00:41:58.448
the immigration and extinction curves, the phase planes,

00:41:58.448 --> 00:42:03.440
and explain what moves each line, you understand the theory, not just its vocabulary.

00:42:03.840 --> 00:42:07.200
The ecological theater is vast, but its rules are learnable.

00:42:07.600 --> 00:42:08.090
Good luck.
